From 5ef27f75bb8c716744b227708344f8471bd4aa45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:58:13 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Let a front-end ask whether the reader needs the password
 first

A reader can refuse every match until the user authenticates with their
password: Touch ID after a restart, as on macOS, or after too many failed
attempts. A verify then ends at once with FP_DEVICE_ERROR_PASSWORD_REQUIRED,
_PASSWORD_REQUIRED_AFTER_BOOT or _LOCKED_OUT, but only after the front-end
has already asked for a finger, so sudo printed "Place your right index
finger on the fingerprint reader" right before "Your password is required
to enable Touch ID."

Add fp_device_get_password_required(), which asks an open device without
waiting whether it refuses to match until the password and why, through a
new optional FpDeviceClass::password_required. Devices without it, and
closed devices, answer FALSE.

The aurora driver answers from SEP_BIO_LOCKOUT_STATE, with the same reasons
as a refused verify; a kernel that cannot say answers FALSE, and the prompt
asks for a finger as before. The virtual device takes SET_PASSWORD_REQUIRED
<FpDeviceError> (0 clears) for front-end tests.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
